  "query_preview": "/WEDNESDAY NIGHT THROUGH SUNDAY/...\nIssued at 215 PM CST Mon Nov 16 2026\n\nThe extended forecast features a significant shift away from surface \nhigh pressure, dry weather, and above-average temperatures. \nBeginning late tonight, an upper-level Pacific trough will move \nonshore and slowly track eastward through the week, reaching the \ndoorstep of the CWA by late Wednesday night or early Thursday \nmorning. The associated surface low is forecast to track through the \nDakotas into southern Canada by Thursday morning, dragging a weak \ncold front toward the region. However, a deep layer of dry air will \nlimit moisture return and keep PoP values minimal ahead of the \nmostly dry frontal passage. Nonetheless, temperatures on Thursday \nwill still climb into the low to mid 60s across the southeastern \nportion of the CWA, while remaining cooler toward the northwest \ncloser to the approaching cold front.\n\nSurface high pressure briefly builds back into the area on Friday, \nwith temperatures rebounding into the low to upper 60s CWA-wide. \nRather than being driven by the departing ridge, this warmth will be \nattributed to the region becoming positioned within the warm sector \nahead of an approaching surface low ejecting off the Rockies. \nAssociated warm air advection and moisture transport will cause \nshower chances to increase late Friday night, initially across the \nnorthwest before quickly spreading eastward overnight and tapering \noff late Saturday night. Following the frontal passage, temperatures \nare forecast to quickly fall into the 40s for highs on Sunday as CAA \nand surface high pressure build into the region to close out the \nweekend.\n\nOverall, the extended period transitions from a dry midweek frontal \npassage to a wetter system ejecting out of the Rockies Friday night \ninto Saturday, followed by a sharp drop back to seasonably cool \ntemperatures in the 40s by Sunday.",
